.SH NAME
ssoossh - The ssoossh client: turns a browser sign-in into a short-lived SSH certificate, from your ssh_config.


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBssoossh [command] [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
The ssoossh client turns a browser sign-in into a short-lived SSH certificate. ssh runs it for you, from ssh_config: it generates a fresh keypair, sends the public key to the ssoossh server, opens your browser so you can sign in and approve, and loads the signed certificate into your ssh-agent, or writes key files when there is no agent. Private keys never leave this machine, and a valid certificate is reused until it expires. It also answers sshd's AuthorizedPrincipalsCommand on hosts (\fBhost\fR) and gets certificates for unattended jobs (\fBservice\fR).

.PP
Start with \fBssoossh ssh config\fR, which prints the ssh_config lines to add.

.PP
Configuration is read from /etc/ssoossh/ssoossh.yaml, then ~/.config/ssoossh.yaml, then the file named by --config (on Windows, %ProgramData%\\ssoossh\\ssoossh.yaml and %AppData%\\ssoossh\\ssoossh.yaml). Flags override the files, except for settings an administrator has locked. -v traces what a command does; --debug prints the resolved settings and where each one came from.

.PP
Documentation: https://mnestor.github.io/ssoossh/


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-c\fP, \fB--config\fP=""
	path to an extra config file, merged after the standard locations

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for ssoossh

.PP
\fB--server\fP=""
	server address, e.g. https://ssh.example.com; https is assumed when no scheme is given

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=0]
	trace what the command is doing, to stderr; repeat for more (-v steps, -vv requests and files, -vvv bodies)


